# Observability / Tracing Rules (LLM Rulebook)

## Purpose

Rules for implementing observability in any service: distributed tracing, logs with trace correlation, metrics exposure. Apply when adding telemetry to services. Traces MUST be visible end-to-end across all services in Grafana/Tempo/Loki/Prometheus.

## Service Identity (MUST)

Every service MUST identify itself in telemetry:

- `service.name` MUST be set and MUST be unique per service/binary.
- Use lowercase kebab-case: `gateway-api`, `users-service`.

```python
# Python
init_tracing("users-service", "http://otel-collector:4317")
```

```typescript
// Node.js/TypeScript
initTracing("users-service", "http://otel-collector:4317")
```

MAY skip in dev: `service.version`, `deployment.environment`.

## Tracing Init (MUST)

Each service MUST:

1. Init OpenTelemetry tracer at startup.
2. Export spans to OpenTelemetry Collector via OTLP gRPC (`:4317`).
3. Install W3C TraceContext propagator.

```python
# Python
from opentelemetry import trace
from opentelemetry.propagate import set_global_textmap
from opentelemetry.propagators.b3 import B3MultiFormat

set_global_textmap(TraceContextTextMapPropagator())
# tracer exports to http://otel-collector:4317 via OTLP gRPC
```

DO NOT:

- Init tracer per request.
- Send traces directly to Tempo from the app (always via Collector).

## Trace Propagation (MUST)

One `trace_id` MUST continue across all internal calls.

- Incoming HTTP/gRPC: extract trace context from headers/metadata.
- Start handler span as CHILD of that context.
- Outgoing calls: inject current span context into headers/metadata.

DO NOT:

- Invent custom headers like `x-request-id` for tracing.
- Use W3C `traceparent` / `tracestate` instead.
- Drop context when relaying calls.

## HTTP Edge Behavior (MUST)

Any HTTP entrypoint (FastAPI, Express, Next.js API routes):

- MUST accept incoming `traceparent` header if present.
- MUST start a span using that as parent.
- MUST pass that context into downstream calls.

MAY skip in local dev: extracting real headers from curl/browser, but still create spans per request.

## Logging Rules (MUST)

- Logs MUST be structured (JSON or key=value).
- Logs MUST include `trace_id` (and SHOULD include `span_id`).
- Logs MUST go to stdout/stderr. No local files.

```python
# Python
logger.info("user login ok", extra={"trace_id": get_trace_id(), "span_id": get_span_id()})
```

```typescript
// TypeScript
logger.info("user login ok", { traceId: getTraceId(), spanId: getSpanId() })
```

DO NOT:

- Write unstructured logs without IDs.
- Hide logs in rotating files.

MAY keep human-readable logs in dev, but production logs MUST include `trace_id`.

## Metrics (SHOULD)

- Every production service SHOULD expose Prometheus metrics.
- MUST expose latency histogram, error count, request count.
- Endpoint SHOULD be `/metrics` on internal port `:9090`.
- Prometheus scrapes; services DO NOT push.

```python
# Python
from prometheus_client import start_http_server
start_http_server(9090)
```

Allowed to skip: helper/batch/cron jobs. Core services MUST expose metrics.

## Span Naming + Attributes (MUST)

Span names MUST describe the operation:

- GOOD: `GET /users/{id}`, `grpc UsersService.GetProfile`, `db.select_user_by_id`
- BAD: `handler`, `stuff`, `call`

Spans SHOULD include:

- `http.method`, `http.route`, `http.status_code`
- `rpc.system`, `rpc.service`, `rpc.method`
- `error = true`, `exception.message` for errors

DO NOT:

- Put secrets or PII in spans.
- Dump raw SQL unless explicitly allowed.

## New Service Checklist

When adding a new service:

```text
[ ] Init tracing with service name to otel-collector:4317
[ ] Install W3C TraceContext propagator
[ ] Extract parent trace (incoming HTTP/gRPC)
[ ] Inject trace (outgoing calls)
[ ] Log to stdout with trace_id
[ ] Expose /metrics on :9090 (or document why not)
```

## Allowed to Skip (Temporarily)

- Prometheus metrics in short-lived scripts.
- Extra labels (user_id, tenant_id).
- `service.version`, `deployment.environment` in dev.

NOT allowed to skip:

- `service.name`
- TraceContext propagation
- Trace context injection/extraction
- Logging `trace_id`

## Out of Scope

- Language-specific framework integrations (covered in stack-specific rules).
- Alerting and dashboard configuration.
- Log aggregation infrastructure setup.
